{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed402918419/4/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"305b1341\" lines=\"22-37\">\n<description>\nExpand each sparse color object into a filled rectangular region using its legend-paired fill color.\n\nInput structure\nA background-color grid has a two-column legend in columns 0 and 1, starting at row 0: each row gives\na source color in the left column and that source color's fill color in the right column.  Outside the\nlegend, the foreground pixels are sparse dot objects whose colors are exactly the legend source colors.\n\nRule\nClear the legend.  For each source color, take the bbox of all of its non-legend dots, expand that bbox\nby one pixel in every direction, and fill background cells inside the expanded rectangle with the paired\nlegend fill color while preserving every original non-legend dot.\n\nKey insight\nThe legend order is also the layer order: earlier legend rows are in front, so fill rectangles are painted\nfrom the last legend row back to the first and higher-priority fills overwrite lower-priority fills.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8abad3cf\" lines=\"10-25\">\n<description>\nRepack each non-background color group as a solid square ordered by increasing size.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one dominant background color, and each other color has a\nperfect-square pixel count. Those square side lengths are unique.\n\nRule\nConvert every non-background color into a filled square whose side length is\nthe square root of its pixel count. Place the squares from left to right in\nincreasing side length, bottom-aligned, with one background column between\nneighboring squares.\n\nKey insight\nThe original geometry of each color is discarded; only the color's total\npixel count determines the size of its packed output square.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"13e47133\" lines=\"56-69\">\n<description>\nFill partitioned regions with concentric colored bands derived from marker pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid partitioned into regions by wall-colored lines.\n- Each region has a dominant base color and a few isolated marker pixels of other colors at varying depths from the region boundary.\n\nRule:\n- For each region, read marker colors ordered by their Chebyshev distance from the boundary to build a color palette.\n- If no marker sits on the boundary itself, the base color leads the palette.\n- Paint every cell with palette[distance mod palette_length], producing repeating concentric rectangular frames that radiate inward.\n\nKey insight: Each marker's depth from the boundary defines its position in the cyclic palette; the pattern then tiles inward automatically.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3e6067c3\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nConnect framed squares via colored bridges following a control-pixel sequence.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Border color forms equal-sized framed squares with unique inner fill colors.\n- Control row below squares: colored pixels read left-to-right encode visitation order.\n\nRule:\n- Start at the square matching first control color c0.\n- For each consecutive pair (ci, ci+1), find the ci+1-colored square aligned in the same row or column band, preferring nearest.\n- Draw a bridge colored ci between the pair, spanning the gap with inner-square thickness.\n\nKey insight: Row/column-band alignment plus proximity resolves ambiguity when inner colors repeat.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"de809cff\" lines=\"86-103\">\n<description>\nTurn black dents in two colored regions into marked opposite-color patches.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with two large foreground colors.\n- The large regions contain small black dents and a few one-cell color specks\n  or protrusions along region boundaries.\n\nRule:\n- Locally clean the two foreground regions: isolated specks become black, and\n  one-cell protrusions surrounded by the other region take that region's color.\n- Each original black dent whose cleaned 3x3 neighborhood is mostly one\n  foreground color becomes the center of a 3x3 patch in the opposite color.\n- The dent center itself is recolored teal (8).\n\nKey insight: The teal pixels mark the original black defects; the surrounding\n3x3 patch uses the other foreground color, after first removing boundary noise.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b1986d4b\" lines=\"19-37\">\n<description>\nRender the filled-square object counts as compact nested stacks ordered by count rank.\n\nInput structure\nA large noisy grid has a dominant background, scattered non-square noise, and\nfilled monochrome square objects. Each square side length has exactly one\nobject color, and side lengths form consecutive sizes.\n\nRule\nIgnore non-square components. If the lowest square in the scene is of the\nsmallest side length, treat that one smallest square as a distractor and do\nnot count it. For count rank k from left to right, create one block from all\nsquare sizes with at least k remaining objects; draw larger squares first and\nsmaller squares over their upper-left corner, then append one background\nseparator column.\n\nKey insight\nThe output is a count histogram of square side lengths, but each histogram\nslot is rendered as nested upper-left square masks using the side colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2e65ae53\" lines=\"88-103\">\n<description>\nFill every lattice slot from the color assigned to that slot position by the other lattices.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The largest foreground color forms several one-pixel-thick rectangular lattice frames.\n- The frame lines divide each lattice into the same row-by-column set of rectangular slots; some slots already contain one non-frame color.\n\nRule:\n- Infer a color for each logical slot position from the colored slots across all lattice frames.\n- Repaint every slot interior in every lattice with the inferred color for its row/column position.\n- Keep the frame lines and all background outside frames unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Slot sizes vary by frame, but the complete color pattern is indexed by lattice slot position rather than by absolute pixel size or location.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8618d23e\" lines=\"9-25\">\n<description>\nStagger the two horizontal input regions onto a larger maroon separator canvas.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a rectangular grid with an even number of rows.\n- The top half and bottom half are the two regions to preserve.\n- Maroon is absent from the input and is used only as the new separator color.\n\nRule:\n- Create an output with one extra row and one extra column, filled with maroon.\n- Copy the top half into the upper-left corner.\n- Copy the bottom half one row below the top region and one column to the right.\n\nKey insight:\nThe output is not a recoloring of either region; it inserts an L-shaped maroon\ngap by diagonally offsetting the lower half from the upper half.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"67e490f4\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes inside a large template frame using shape-matched outside sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. One large monochrome frame object (template).\n- Inside the frame: holes of various shapes (non-frame-color regions).\n- Outside the frame: small monochrome sprites scattered on bg.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the frame's bounding box; fill entirely with frame color.\n- For each hole, find an outside sprite whose shape matches (rotation/flip allowed).\n- Fill the hole with the matched sprite's color. If multiple sprites match, use the most frequent color.\n\nKey insight: Sprites act as shape-coded color keys — their shape identifies which hole they fill, their color determines the fill color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2c181942\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nAttach monochrome sprites to the matching sides of a central hollow square.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A hollow NxN square (hub) with bg corners/interior and four differently-colored edges (top, bottom, left, right).\n- 2-4 monochrome sprites scattered on the grid, each matching one hub edge color.\n\nRule:\n- For each hub edge color, find the matching sprite outside the hub.\n- Rotate the sprite (0/90/180/270°) so its contact edge has exactly N-2 contiguous cells aligned with the hub side, and the rotated sprite is mirror-symmetric along the contact-edge axis (UD for left/right, LR for top/bottom).\n- Translate it to adjoin that hub side. \n- Clear everything else to bg; output is hub + attached sprites only.\n\nKey insight: Each edge color uniquely pairs a sprite to a side; symmetry along the contact-edge axis disambiguates the correct rotation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1d61978c\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nRecolor the two diagonal foreground-bar orientations, assigning teal to the larger orientation and red to the smaller one.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground pixels form one-cell-thick diagonal bars.\n- Every foreground pixel belongs to exactly one diagonal orientation.\n\nRule\n- Keep the background unchanged.\n- Classify each foreground pixel by whether it has a diagonal neighbor on the NW/SE axis or the NE/SW axis.\n- Recolor the orientation with more foreground pixels teal and the other orientation red.\n\nKey insight\n- The output colors depend on the relative population of the two diagonal orientations, not on a fixed slash direction or position.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
